Terminate a line read without editing after removing its CR.
When standard input is not a console, read_line_input reads a line with
fgets and removes the CR of a CR LF ending. remove_cr moves the text in
place and returns the shorter length but leaves the old end alone, so
the string kept a stray copy of its last character: ".\r\n" became
".\n\n". The returned length was right, but code that looks at the
string itself saw the extra newline. In ed, a lone period ended by CR LF
was not taken as the end of text input, so the commands after it, such
as w and q, were added to the buffer as text and the file was never
written. Write a new terminator at the shortened length.

---
 libbb/lineedit.c |    1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

--- a/libbb/lineedit.c
+++ b/libbb/lineedit.c
@@ -2725,6 +2725,7 @@
 			len = strlen(command);
 #if ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
 			len = remove_cr(command, len);
+			command[len] = '\0';
 #endif
 		}
 		DEINIT_S();
